In cross-platform environments (such as Windows), files checked out with git may have CRLF (\r\n) line endings. Comparing raw file contents strictly with existing === content will fail because the generated content uses LF (\n) line endings. To ensure portability and prevent false check failures, normalize line endings by replacing \r\n with \n when reading existing files.

```suggestion
function existingFile(root, relPath) {
  const target = path.join(root, relPath);
  if (!fs.existsSync(target)) return null;
  return fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8').replace(/\r\n/g, '\n');
}
